1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,267
In 1950, a baby born in Spain could expect to live about 62 years.

2
00:00:07,267 --> 00:00:10,400
A baby born in Norway, almost 72.

3
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:17,097
Today, Spain is one of the longest-living countries in the world, at almost 84.

4
00:00:17,097 --> 00:00:18,900
And it's ahead of Norway.

5
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:22,896
This is how southern Europe overtook the north,

6
00:00:22,896 --> 00:00:25,700
and what happened in the east along the way.

7
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,235
First, what the number means.

8
00:00:29,235 --> 00:00:34,305
Life expectancy at birth is how long a baby born in a given year would live,

9
00:00:34,305 --> 00:00:38,375
if the death rates of that year stayed the same all its life.

10
00:00:39,375 --> 00:00:41,658
So it isn't a forecast for anyone.

11
00:00:41,658 --> 00:00:45,485
It's a snapshot of how healthy a country is in that year.

12
00:00:45,485 --> 00:00:49,580
And when many babies die young, the average falls a long way,

13
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:52,400
which is why the early figures are so low.

14
00:00:53,400 --> 00:00:57,921
The figures come from the United Nations' World Population Prospects,

15
00:00:57,921 --> 00:01:02,245
through Our World in Data. Each square on this map is one country,

16
00:01:02,245 --> 00:01:05,325
and the brighter it is, the longer people live.

17
00:01:06,725 --> 00:01:15,087
1950. Europe as a whole: 62 years, well above the world average of 46. At the top:

18
00:01:15,087 --> 00:01:23,450
Norway at 71.6, the Netherlands at 71.4 and Sweden at 71.1, all in the north-west.

19
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:33,823
Further south and east, the numbers are much lower. Italy, 65.7. Spain, 61.8.

20
00:01:33,823 --> 00:01:37,475
Portugal, 58.5. Albania, 44.5.

21
00:01:38,475 --> 00:01:43,350
Between the top and the bottom of this map, that's a gap of 27 years.

22
00:01:44,750 --> 00:01:47,780
Much of that gap is children. In 1950,

23
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:53,362
about one child in ten in Spain died before turning five. In Portugal,

24
00:01:53,362 --> 00:01:56,950
about one in seven. In Norway, one in thirty.

25
00:01:58,550 --> 00:02:03,248
Then comes the long climb. Through the 1950s, 60s and 70s,

26
00:02:03,248 --> 00:02:06,650
almost every country adds years, and fast.

27
00:02:09,650 --> 00:02:15,797
Far fewer children die young. Vaccines and antibiotics reach almost everyone.

28
00:02:15,797 --> 00:02:20,268
Clean water and better food do the rest. In Spain today,

29
00:02:20,268 --> 00:02:24,100
about one child in 300 dies before turning five.

30
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:29,962
By 1975, Europe's average has reached almost 71,

31
00:02:29,962 --> 00:02:33,750
nearly as high as Norway's was in 1950.

32
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:40,175
Spain is climbing fastest of all. It has already passed Germany and the UK.

33
00:02:41,775 --> 00:02:45,413
And in 1982, Spain passes Norway. Since 1950,

34
00:02:45,413 --> 00:02:51,800
it has added about five and a half months of life expectancy every single year.

35
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:55,225
By 1990, Europe's average is 73,

36
00:02:55,225 --> 00:03:01,591
and the gap between the top and the bottom of the map has shrunk to about ten years.

37
00:03:01,591 --> 00:03:07,275
Portugal, the lowest in western Europe in 1950, has gained almost 16 years.

38
00:03:08,675 --> 00:03:17,543
But watch the east. As the Soviet Union breaks up, life expectancy in Russia falls,

39
00:03:17,543 --> 00:03:21,175
from 69.3 in 1990 to 63.8 in 1994.

40
00:03:22,575 --> 00:03:31,900
The Baltic states fall too. Latvia drops from 69.5 in 1990 to 65.2 in 1994.

41
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:36,885
By 2000, Russia is still only at 65. Ukraine,

42
00:03:36,885 --> 00:03:40,839
Belarus and Moldova are lower than in 1990 too.

43
00:03:40,839 --> 00:03:47,400
Researchers link it to economic collapse, alcohol and strained health systems.

44
00:03:48,400 --> 00:03:55,245
For a decade, the gap between east and west grows instead of shrinking.

45
00:03:55,245 --> 00:04:01,125
Spain and Russia were 7.7 years apart in 1990. By 2000, 14.4.

46
00:04:02,725 --> 00:04:05,625
Meanwhile, the south keeps on climbing.

47
00:04:09,825 --> 00:04:16,609
By 2019, the top three on our map are Switzerland at 83.8, Spain at 83.

48
00:04:16,609 --> 00:04:23,775
5 and Italy at 83.3. Spain started ten years behind Norway. Now it's ahead.

49
00:04:25,375 --> 00:04:30,135
Italy did the same, passing Norway in the late 1980s. In 2019,

50
00:04:30,135 --> 00:04:35,125
Switzerland is the only country on the map ahead of both of them.

51
00:04:36,325 --> 00:04:43,800
Portugal, which started at 58.5, passed Germany in 2010 and the UK in 2014.

52
00:04:45,400 --> 00:04:49,625
Then comes the pandemic. Here are the last few years, close up.

53
00:04:52,425 --> 00:04:58,609
Europe's average falls from 78.8 in 2019 to 77.0 in 2021,

54
00:04:58,609 --> 00:05:04,250
the biggest fall since these figures begin, in 1950.

55
00:05:05,250 --> 00:05:12,425
Some countries lose much more. Bulgaria drops by 3.7 years, and Russia by 3.3.

56
00:05:13,425 --> 00:05:18,127
Others barely notice it. Norway's figure doesn't fall at all,

57
00:05:18,127 --> 00:05:23,600
which is why Norway was briefly back ahead of Spain, from 2020 to 2022.

58
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:32,650
Then it recovers. By 2023, Europe is back to 79.1, higher than before the pandemic.

59
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:42,481
2023. Switzerland, 84.0. Italy and Spain, 83.7. Then France, Norway,

60
00:05:42,481 --> 00:05:46,475
Malta and Sweden, all about 83.3.

61
00:05:47,475 --> 00:05:54,456
Further down: Germany at 81.4 and the UK at 81.3. Both are now behind Spain,

62
00:05:54,456 --> 00:05:59,325
Italy and France, and behind Portugal and Greece too.

63
00:06:00,325 --> 00:06:07,876
Then Poland, 78.6. Hungary, 77.0. Romania, 75.9. Bulgaria, 75.6.

64
00:06:07,876 --> 00:06:16,724
And lowest on the map: Ukraine at 73.4, Russia at 73.2 and Moldova at 71.2.

65
00:06:16,724 --> 00:06:24,275
For comparison, the world average is 73.2, the same as Russia's.

66
00:06:25,275 --> 00:06:31,212
Since 1950, Spain has gained almost 22 years, and Portugal almost 24.

67
00:06:31,212 --> 00:06:36,375
The biggest gain on the map is Albania's: 35 years. Albania,

68
00:06:36,375 --> 00:06:43,000
at the very bottom in 1950, now lives longer than Poland, Hungary or Croatia.

69
00:06:44,600 --> 00:06:50,342
Why the south? Researchers point to several things, and no single one explains it:

70
00:06:50,342 --> 00:06:55,104
lower rates of heart disease, diet, strong family ties into old age,

71
00:06:55,104 --> 00:06:58,325
and health systems that reach almost everyone.

72
00:06:59,325 --> 00:07:02,871
And Germany is the puzzle. It spends about 8,

73
00:07:02,871 --> 00:07:09,411
300 dollars a year per person on health, adjusted for prices. Spain spends about 4,

74
00:07:09,411 --> 00:07:15,400
900. Yet a baby born in Spain can expect to live more than two years longer.

75
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:20,154
Three things to take away. One:

76
00:07:20,154 --> 00:07:28,700
Europe as a whole gained 17 years of life expectancy, from 62 in 1950 to 79 in 2023.

77
00:07:29,700 --> 00:07:35,565
Two: the south overtook the north. Spain and Italy now live longer than Norway,

78
00:07:35,565 --> 00:07:36,975
Sweden and Germany.

79
00:07:37,975 --> 00:07:43,269
Three: the east fell behind in the 1990s, Russia by more than five years,

80
00:07:43,269 --> 00:07:45,300
and it is still catching up.

81
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:52,150
Next on Terrachart: who works the longest hours in Europe. It's not who you think.

82
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:55,412
Sources are in the description.

83
00:07:55,412 --> 00:08:00,003
Why do you think Spain and Italy live longer than Germany and the UK?

84
00:08:00,003 --> 00:08:01,600
Tell us in the comments.
